.TH BTRFS-PATROL 8 "2026-09-24" "btrfs-patrol 0.8.0" "System Administration"
.\" Left-aligned and unhyphenated, so commands, paths and package names stay whole.
.\" The man macros reapply these at every paragraph, from groff's AD string and HY register.
.ds AD l
.nr HY 0
.ad l
.nh
.
.SH NAME
btrfs-patrol \- btrfs snapshot manager and rollback tool for Fedora
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B btrfs-patrol
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-color
.IR WHEN ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.PP
.B btrfs-patrol
.B \-\-version
.PP
.B btrfs-patrol
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.B \-\-help
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B btrfs-patrol
takes, lists and prunes read-only snapshots of the btrfs subvolume mounted at
.IR / ,
takes a snapshot before each
.BR dnf5 (8)
transaction and once a day, and rolls the system back to an earlier snapshot
with one command.
.PP
It can manage other subvolumes too, such as
.IR /home :
they get snapshots of their own, and each is rolled back on its own; see
.BR "OTHER SUBVOLUMES" .
.PP
A rollback replaces the root subvolume with a writable copy of the chosen
snapshot. The system you leave is not lost: it is kept as a new snapshot of
kind
.BR rollback ,
so you can roll forward to it again. Every step of a rollback is checked
first, and if a step fails, the steps already done are undone. Nothing
reboots on its own; the restored system starts at the next boot.
.PP
It works with Fedora's layout: the root subvolume is found at boot through
the btrfs default subvolume or with
.BR subvol= ,
kernels live in a separate
.IR /boot ,
and the root may be on LUKS and LVM.
.PP
Snapshots are not backups. They live on the same filesystem as the system
they protect; see
.BR CAVEATS .
.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIPATH\fR"
Use the configuration file
.I PATH
instead of
.BR $BTRFS_PATROL_CONFIG ,
or, when that is not set,
.IR /etc/btrfs\-patrol/config.toml .
.TP
.BR \-\-color " \fIWHEN\fR"
Colors in the output:
.B auto
(on a terminal only, and not when
.B NO_COLOR
is set or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ),
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
Overrides
.B output.color
in the configuration.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help for
.B btrfs-patrol
or for one command, and exit.
.
.SH COMMANDS
Every command except
.B list
and
.B check
must be run as root.
.B list
and
.B check
read the snapshots directory, which is readable only by root, so in practice
they need root too.
.
.TP
.B setup \fR[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Set the system up for
.BR btrfs-patrol .
Run it once after installing. It works out what is missing, shows it, asks
for confirmation, and then does only the missing steps, so it is safe to run
again \- also after a step failed:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Write the configuration file, if there is none, with the root subvolume
detected from the mount at
.IR / .
An existing configuration is kept.
.IP 2. 4
Create a top-level subvolume for the snapshots
.RB ( snapshots ),
next to the root subvolume and readable only by root, because old snapshots
can contain setuid programs with known security holes.
.IP 3. 4
Create the mount point
.RI ( /.snapshots ),
add it to
.I /etc/fstab
with the root's mount options, after saving the old file as
.IR /etc/fstab.btrfs\-patrol\-backup ,
and mount it.
.IP 4. 4
When an SELinux policy is installed, add the snapshots directory to
.IR /etc/selinux/fixfiles_exclude_dirs ;
when SELinux is enabled, give the snapshots directory and its entries their
labels. See
.BR SELINUX .
.IP 5. 4
Enable and start the daily snapshot timer, if its unit is installed and
disabled. A masked timer is left alone.
.RE
.IP
Afterwards it runs
.BR check .
It refuses rather than guesses when something is in the way: a root that is
not btrfs or is the top-level subvolume, a configuration for a different root
subvolume, something else mounted at the snapshots directory, a non-empty
snapshots directory, or an
.I /etc/fstab
line that mounts it differently.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what setup would do, and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.RE
.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fISELECTOR\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
List snapshots, or only those matching
.I SELECTOR
(see
.BR SELECTORS ),
as a table of ID, date, time, kernel, kind and description, with a SUBVOLUME
column when other subvolumes are configured. A
.B *
before an ID marks a kept snapshot. On a terminal, long descriptions are cut
to fit its width; written to a pipe or a file, they are printed whole.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Wrap long descriptions onto further lines instead of cutting them.
.RE
.
.TP
.B snapshot \fR[\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-description\fR \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-k\fR|\fB\-\-keep\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-subvolume\fR \fINAME\fR]...
Take read-only snapshots, of kind
.BR manual ,
of the root subvolume and of every subvolume in the configuration, one
snapshot and ID each, then prune each subvolume (see
.BR prune ).
Refused when one of them has a rollback waiting for its reboot.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " \fITEXT\fR"
A description to remember the snapshots by.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep
Keep the snapshots: never prune them.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-subvolume " \fINAME\fR"
Only snapshot the subvolume
.IR NAME :
.B root
or the name of a
.BI [subvolumes. NAME ]
table. Repeat it for more than one.
.RE
.
.TP
.B describe \fIID\fR \fIDESCRIPTION\fR
Replace the description of snapshot
.IR ID .
.
.TP
.B keep \fISELECTOR\fR
Keep the matching snapshots: pruning never deletes them, and they do not count
toward their subvolume's
.BR max_snapshots .
.
.TP
.B unkeep \fISELECTOR\fR
Let the matching snapshots be pruned again.
.
.TP
.B delete \fISELECTOR\fR [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Show the matching snapshots, ask for confirmation, and delete them \- kept
snapshots included. A snapshot's ID is not reused after it is deleted.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.RE
.
.TP
.B prune
Delete each subvolume's oldest snapshots that are not kept, until at most its
.B max_snapshots
of them remain:
.B retention.max_snapshots
for root, and the value in each
.BI [subvolumes. NAME ]
table for the others. Snapshots of a subvolume that is no longer in the
configuration are never pruned. Taking a manual, timer or dnf snapshot prunes
that subvolume too.
.
.TP
.B prune\-kernels [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Remove the boot entries of kernels that
.I /usr/lib/modules
has no modules for, using
.BR kernel\-install (8).
A rollback restores the modules but not
.IR /boot ,
so a snapshot taken before a kernel was installed leaves that kernel's entry
behind, offering a boot with no drivers to load. The running kernel is never
touched, and
.B \-\-dry\-run
lists the entries without removing them. An entry that
.BR kernel\-install (8)
does not manage is left in place and reported, because kernel\-install succeeds
whether or not it removed anything. Rolling forward to a snapshot that
did have those kernels needs
.B "kernel\-install add\-all"
to put their entries back.
.
.TP
.B rollback \fIID\fR [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Roll the subvolume snapshot
.I ID
is of back to it \- the root subvolume, or another one in the configuration \-
then reboot to use it. It shows the snapshot and the plan, prints any
warnings, and asks for confirmation. See
.B ROLLBACK
and
.BR "OTHER SUBVOLUMES" .
.RS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Run every check and show the plan, and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.RE
.
.TP
.B convert \fIPATH\fR [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-config\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Turn the existing directory
.I PATH
into a btrfs subvolume, so it can be snapshotted and rolled back on its own
instead of going wherever the root subvolume goes. The new subvolume is
nested where the directory was, so no
.BR fstab (5)
entry and no mount unit are needed, and snapshots of root stop including it.
.RS
.PP
The contents are copied with reflinks, so the copy shares its extents with the
original and costs almost no space. The directory is then moved aside and the
subvolume put in its place; the previous contents are kept, not deleted, and
their location is printed.
.PP
Processes that already have files open under the directory keep writing to the
old copy until they reopen it \- systemd\-journald being the obvious one. They
are listed afterwards with a
.B systemctl restart
line for the services that accept one. A unit that refuses a manual restart -
.BR auditd (8)
sets RefuseManualStart and holds
.IR /var/log ,
so this is the ordinary case - is named separately, because including it makes
the whole command fail and reopens nothing; only a reboot moves it across.
Nothing is restarted automatically.
.PP
It refuses
.IR / ,
.IR /boot ,
.IR /etc ,
.IR /usr
and the pseudo\-filesystems, a path that is already a subvolume, a mount point,
and anything not on the btrfs filesystem mounted at
.IR / .
.TP
.BR \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Name for the subvolume in the configuration and in
.BR list .
The default comes from the path:
.I /var/log
becomes
.IR var\-log .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not add a
.BI [subvolumes. NAME ]
table to the configuration. The table is printed instead, to paste in by hand.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Run every check and show the plan, and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.RE
.
.TP
.B diff \fIOLD\fR \fINEW\fR
Show what changed between two snapshots of the same subvolume: files added,
removed, modified and renamed, and those whose permissions, ownership or
extended attributes changed.
.RS
.PP
It asks btrfs itself, with
.B "btrfs send \-\-no\-data"
piped into
.BR "btrfs receive \-\-dump" ,
so no file contents are read and the cost is in metadata rather than in the
size of the tree. Both snapshots must be of the same subvolume; either order
works, and reversing them reverses the answer.
.PP
Timestamps alone are not a change. Running a program updates its access time,
so a comparison across any real work would otherwise be mostly binaries that
were merely executed.
.PP
Output is one path per line, marked
.B +
added,
.B \-
removed,
.B ~
modified,
.B >
renamed and
.B a
attributes.
.RE
.
.TP
.B tui
Browse and manage snapshots in a full\-screen interface. The snapshot list of
every configured subvolume, the detail of the highlighted one, and the keys to
take, keep, describe and delete snapshots.
.RS
.PP
Keys: arrows,
.BR PgUp / PgDn ,
.BR Home / End
move;
.B Tab
changes subvolume;
.B Enter
shows one snapshot in full;
.B n
takes a snapshot of the subvolume being shown;
.B d
changes a description;
.B k
keeps or unkeeps;
.B x
deletes, after confirming;
.B R
rolls back to the highlighted snapshot, showing the plan and its checks first;
.B c
marks a snapshot and then compares it with another;
.B /
filters with a selector, as
.B list
takes;
.B r
reloads;
.B ?
lists the keys;
.B q
quits.
.PP
It enforces the same rules the commands do: a snapshot is not taken while a
rollback is waiting for its reboot or while the snapshots subvolume is not
mounted, and the snapshot the system is currently running from is not deleted.
.PP
Every command above is reachable from it except
.BR setup ,
.B check
and
.BR prune .
.RE
.
.TP
.B check
Compare the configuration with what is mounted, and look for broken
snapshots: a root that is not btrfs or is not the configured root subvolume,
a snapshots directory that is not mounted, is another subvolume or is on
another filesystem, and snapshots with unreadable metadata or without their
subvolume. These are reported as errors. It warns, without failing, when a
rollback is waiting for its reboot, and about SELinux labels or the relabel
exclusion missing.
.
.TP
.B dnf\-hook pre\fR|\fBpost
Take a snapshot for a dnf5 transaction. It is run by the
.BR libdnf5\-actions (8)
plugin, not by hand, and refuses to run from a terminal. See
.BR "DNF TRANSACTIONS" .
.
.SH SELECTORS
.BR list ,
.BR keep ,
.B unkeep
and
.B delete
take a selector:
.TP
.IR ID [ , ID ...]
Snapshot IDs and ranges, separated by commas, such as
.BR 3 ,
.BR 1,10,20\-23 .
An ID that does not exist is an error; a range selects the snapshots that
exist in it, in either order.
.TP
.BI date= TEXT
The date, as
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD ,
contains
.IR TEXT ,
such as
.B date=2026\-09
for September 2026.
.TP
.BI time= TEXT
The time, as
.IR HH:MM:SS ,
contains
.IR TEXT ,
such as
.BR time=16: .
.TP
.BI kernel= TEXT
The kernel the snapshot was taken on contains
.IR TEXT ,
such as
.BR kernel=7.2 .
.TP
.BI kind= TEXT
The kind contains
.IR TEXT ,
such as
.B kind=dnf
for both dnf kinds. The kinds are
.B manual
.RB ( snapshot ),
.B timer
(the daily timer),
.B dnf\-pre
and
.B dnf\-post
(around a dnf transaction), and
.B rollback
(the state a rollback left).
.TP
.BI description= TEXT
The description contains
.IR TEXT .
.TP
.BI subvolume= NAME
Snapshots of the subvolume
.IR NAME ,
such as
.B subvolume=home
or
.BR subvolume=root .
Unlike the other fields, the whole name has to match.
.TP
.BR keep=yes ", " keep=no
Kept snapshots, or the others.
.PP
Text matches ignore case. A field match cannot be combined with other terms,
so its text may contain commas. Quote selectors that contain spaces or shell
characters, such as
.BR "description=\(dqbefore the upgrade\(dq" .
.
.SH ROLLBACK
This section is about rolling back the root subvolume; for the others, see
.BR "OTHER SUBVOLUMES" .
Before changing anything,
.B rollback
checks that:
.IP \(bu 2
the snapshot has kernel modules for the running kernel, since the restored
system could not load its drivers otherwise;
.IP \(bu 2
the running kernel has a boot entry, because
.I /boot
is not rolled back: a Boot Loader Specification Type #1 entry in
.IR /boot/loader/entries ,
or a unified kernel image (Type #2) in
.I EFI/Linux
on the EFI system partition mounted at
.IR /boot ,
.I /efi
or
.IR /boot/efi .
An image's kernel version is read from its
.B .uname
section, from the Linux kernel's own header, or last from the file name
.BR kernel\-install (8)
gives it;
.IP \(bu 2
the root is found at boot by name
.RB ( subvol= )
or through the btrfs default subvolume, and not by subvolume ID
.RB ( subvolid= ),
which cannot follow a rollback; the snapshot's
.I /etc/fstab
and the kernel command line are read for this;
.IP \(bu 2
the configured root and snapshots subvolumes are the ones mounted.
.PP
It warns when other boot entries have kernels the snapshot has no modules
for; choose the running kernel in the boot menu in that case. Then it:
.IP 1. 4
creates a snapshot entry of kind
.BR rollback ,
kept, for the current state;
.IP 2. 4
moves the current root subvolume into that entry \- the running system keeps
using it until the reboot;
.IP 3. 4
creates a writable snapshot of the chosen snapshot as the new root subvolume;
.IP 4. 4
moves subvolumes nested inside the old root, such as
.IR /var/lib/portables ,
into the new one;
.IP 5. 4
if the root is found through the default subvolume, points the default at the
new root and reads it back.
.PP
.B Rolled back:
everything in the root subvolume, including
.IR /etc ,
.I /usr
and the RPM database.
.B Not rolled back:
.I /home
and every other separate subvolume, and
.IR /boot .
.B Kept:
nested subvolumes, moved into the restored root.
.PP
.I /var/log
is part of the root subvolume, so the journal written since the snapshot stays
with the previous state;
.B rollback
prints the
.BR journalctl (1)
.B \-D
command that reads it.
.PP
Until the reboot,
.I /
is still the previous system:
.B check
says so,
.B snapshot
refuses, the timer and the dnf hook skip their snapshots, a second
.B rollback
is refused, and whatever is installed or changed in the meantime is lost at
the reboot. Hibernating instead of rebooting is safe: the rollback takes
effect at the next real boot.
.PP
When you are happy with the restored system, delete the
.B rollback
snapshot to free its space, or roll back to it to undo the rollback.
.
.SH OTHER SUBVOLUMES
Besides the root subvolume,
.B btrfs-patrol
takes snapshots of the other btrfs subvolumes named by
.BI [subvolumes. NAME ]
tables in the configuration \- such as
.I /home
on a Fedora install, or
.I /var/log
once it is a subvolume of its own. They have to be on the same btrfs
filesystem as the root. Their snapshots share root's snapshot IDs, list and
selectors, are shown with their subvolume's name in
.BR list ,
and are pruned against each subvolume's own
.BR max_snapshots .
.PP
Each subvolume is rolled back on its own:
.B rollback
restores the subvolume the chosen snapshot is of, and leaves every other one
as it is. Rolling back root does not touch
.IR /home ,
and rolling back
.I /home
does not touch the system. The steps are the ones root's rollback takes \- the
current state is kept as a
.B rollback
snapshot, nested subvolumes move into the restored one, and a failed step is
undone \- without root's kernel and boot entry checks, which only root needs.
The subvolume has to be found by its name at boot:
.IP \(bu 2
a subvolume mounted on its own, as Fedora mounts
.IR /home ,
must be mounted from
.I /etc/fstab
with
.BR subvol= ,
not
.BR subvolid= .
It stays mounted as it was until the reboot, like root.
.IP \(bu 2
a subvolume reached through its path inside another one, such as
.I /var/log
inside root, is replaced at once: files opened from then on are the restored
ones, while programs that hold files open there keep the previous ones until
they restart. Restart them, or reboot.
.PP
A subvolume inside the root subvolume is not part of root's snapshots, so a
rollback of root does not roll it back, and
.B check
warns about it. That is the point for
.IR /var/log ,
but a subvolume at
.I /var/lib
would keep its data \- Flatpak applications, containers, the state of
services \- through a rollback of root.
.PP
The path in a table has to be a subvolume already:
.B btrfs-patrol
does not turn a directory into one.
.
.SH DNF TRANSACTIONS
With
.B libdnf5\-plugin\-actions
installed, the actions file
.I /etc/dnf/libdnf5\-plugins/actions.d/btrfs\-patrol.actions
runs
.B btrfs\-patrol dnf\-hook pre
before and
.B btrfs\-patrol dnf\-hook post
after each transaction on the running system (not with
.BR \-\-installroot ).
The hook uses the plugin's json communication mode to ask which packages the
transaction changes, so the snapshot's description says what changed, such as
.RB \(lq "upgrade kernel\-core, mesa\-dri\-drivers +12 more; install tree" \(rq.
.PP
By default only the
.B dnf\-pre
snapshot is taken; set
.B dnf.post_snapshot
to also take one after, or
.B dnf.pre_snapshot
to false for none. Other subvolumes are in dnf's snapshots only when their
table says
.BR "dnf = true" .
Like every snapshot, a dnf snapshot prunes its subvolume's oldest snapshots
beyond its
.BR max_snapshots .
The hook does nothing until
.B setup
has written the configuration, skips its snapshot while a rollback is waiting
for its reboot, and never makes a transaction fail: problems are logged to
.I /var/log/dnf5.log
and repeated on standard error.
.
.SH SCHEDULED SNAPSHOTS
.I btrfs\-patrol\-snapshot.timer
starts
.I btrfs\-patrol\-snapshot.service
once a day, shortly after midnight (up to 15 minutes later, at random), which
takes snapshots of kind
.B timer
of root and of every other subvolume whose table doesn't say
.BR "timer = false" ,
described as
.RB \(lq "Scheduled snapshot" \(rq.
A day missed because the machine was off is made up at the next boot. While a
rollback is waiting for its reboot, the service skips its snapshot and still
succeeds.
.PP
The package installs the timer disabled, as Fedora does for every package's
units, and
.B setup
enables it. Show when it runs next, or turn it off, with:
.PP
.RS
.EX
systemctl list\-timers btrfs\-patrol\-snapshot.timer
sudo systemctl disable \-\-now btrfs\-patrol\-snapshot.timer
.EE
.RE
.PP
A timer turned off with
.B systemctl mask
stays off:
.B setup
enables only a timer that is disabled.
.
.SH SELINUX
A full SELinux relabel, such as the one
.BR fixfiles (8)
runs for
.IR /.autorelabel ,
gives everything below the snapshots directory the policy's label for it.
Read-only snapshots cannot be changed, but a
.B rollback
snapshot is the previous root subvolume and is writable: after a relabel,
rolling back to it would boot a system whose files all have the wrong label.
.B setup
therefore adds the snapshots directory to
.I /etc/selinux/fixfiles_exclude_dirs
whenever a policy is installed, even with SELinux off, because turning SELinux
on is exactly when a relabel runs. For the same reason, run
.BR restorecon (8)
on the whole system as
.BR "restorecon \-R \-e /.snapshots /" .
.PP
A new btrfs subvolume has no label, so when SELinux is enabled,
.B setup
labels the snapshots directory, its entry directories and their metadata with
.BR restorecon ,
never recursively: what is inside a snapshot keeps the labels of the system it
was taken from. Entries created later inherit the directory's label.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a TOML file,
.I /etc/btrfs\-patrol/config.toml
by default, written by
.BR setup .
Unknown sections or options are errors. Every option is optional; the
defaults match Fedora's installer.
.TP
.B [filesystem]
.RS
.TP
.BR device " = " \(dqauto\(dq
The block device of the btrfs filesystem, such as
.I /dev/nvme0n1p3
or
.IR /dev/mapper/luks\-UUID ,
or
.B auto
for the device mounted at
.IR / .
.TP
.BR root_subvolume " = " \(dqroot\(dq
The subvolume mounted at
.IR / ,
as a path relative to the top-level subvolume.
.TP
.BR snapshots_subvolume " = " \(dqsnapshots\(dq
The top-level subvolume that holds the snapshots, relative to the top-level
subvolume. It must not be inside the root subvolume, or a rollback would move
the snapshots away together with the root.
.TP
.BR snapshots_dir " = " \(dq/.snapshots\(dq
Where
.B snapshots_subvolume
is mounted; an absolute path.
.RE
.TP
.B [retention]
.RS
.TP
.BR max_snapshots " = " 50
How many snapshots that are not kept to leave when pruning; at least 1.
.RE
.TP
.B [boot]
.RS
.TP
.BR entries " = " 0
How many of the newest snapshots get a boot menu entry, so a system too
broken to log in to can be booted from a snapshot and rolled back from
there. Zero, the default, writes none: the boot menu is shared with the
rest of the system. An entry is only written for a snapshot whose kernel
is still installed and whose modules the snapshot has, and only the
entries this program wrote are ever changed or removed. They boot the
snapshot read\-only, which leaves it unaltered, and
.B rollback
works from there.
.RE
.TP
.B [dnf]
.RS
.TP
.BR pre_snapshot " = " true
Take a snapshot before each dnf5 transaction.
.TP
.BR post_snapshot " = " false
Also take one after each transaction.
.RE
.TP
.B [output]
.RS
.TP
.BR color " = " \(dqauto\(dq
.BR auto ,
.B always
or
.BR never ,
as for
.BR \-\-color .
.RE
.TP
.BI [subvolumes. NAME ]
One table for each other subvolume to take snapshots of; see
.BR "OTHER SUBVOLUMES" .
.I NAME
is what
.B list
shows and
.BI subvolume= NAME
selects: letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B .
and
.BR \- ,
but not
.BR root .
.RS
.TP
.BR path " = " \(dq/home\(dq
Required. Where the subvolume is: its mount point, or its path inside another
subvolume. Neither
.I /
nor a path inside the snapshots directory.
.TP
.BR max_snapshots " = " \fIretention.max_snapshots\fR
How many of its snapshots that are not kept to leave when pruning; at least 1.
.TP
.BR timer " = " true
Whether the daily timer takes a snapshot of it.
.TP
.BR dnf " = " false
Whether dnf transactions take a snapshot of it too.
.RE
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/btrfs\-patrol/config.toml
The configuration.
.TP
.I /.snapshots/ID/snapshot
Snapshot
.IR ID :
a read-only btrfs snapshot, or for kind
.B rollback
the writable previous root subvolume.
.TP
.I /.snapshots/ID/info.json
Its metadata: format version, creation time, kernel, kind, description,
whether it is kept and, for a subvolume other than root, the subvolume's name.
Snapshots of other subvolumes use format 2, which versions before 0.3 refuse
to read, so they can never roll root back to a snapshot of
.IR /home .
.TP
.I /.snapshots/.next\-id
The ID the next snapshot gets. It only moves forward, so IDs are never
reused.
.TP
.I /.snapshots/.lock
Held while the snapshots are being changed, so a dnf transaction and the
timer cannot pick the same ID or prune each other's snapshots.
.TP
.I /etc/fstab.btrfs\-patrol\-backup
The copy of
.I /etc/fstab
from before
.B setup
added its line.
.TP
.I /etc/dnf/libdnf5\-plugins/actions.d/btrfs\-patrol.actions
The dnf5 hook.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/btrfs\-patrol\-snapshot.timer
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/btrfs\-patrol\-snapshot.service
The daily snapshot.
.TP
.I /etc/selinux/fixfiles_exclude_dirs
Directories full relabels skip; see
.BR SELINUX .
.TP
.I /var/log/dnf5.log
Where the dnf hook logs its snapshots and problems.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BTRFS_PATROL_CONFIG
The configuration file to use, unless
.B \-\-config
is given.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, no colors with
.BR "\-\-color auto" .
.TP
.B TERM
When
.BR dumb ,
no colors with
.BR "\-\-color auto" .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, a dry run, or nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
An error, a problem found by
.BR check ,
or a confirmation that was declined.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line, such as an unknown command or option.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted with Ctrl+C.
.PP
.B dnf\-hook
exits 0 even when its snapshot fails, so that a transaction never fails
because of it.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set a system up, after seeing what will be done:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol setup \-\-dry\-run
sudo btrfs\-patrol setup
.EE
.RE
.PP
Take and keep a snapshot before a risky change:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol snapshot \-d "before the new kernel" \-\-keep
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the snapshots dnf took, those taken in September 2026, and those taken
before installing a package called tree:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol list kind=dnf
sudo btrfs\-patrol list date=2026\-09
sudo btrfs\-patrol list description=tree
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep snapshots 12 and 20 to 23, and delete snapshots 1 to 10:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol keep 12,20\-23
sudo btrfs\-patrol list 1\-10
sudo btrfs\-patrol delete 1\-10
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B delete
takes kept snapshots too, so check what a selector matches with
.B list
first. To be rid of old snapshots without touching kept ones, lower
.B retention.max_snapshots
and run
.BR prune .
.PP
Take snapshots of
.I /home
too, keeping 20 of them: add a table to
.IR /etc/btrfs\-patrol/config.toml ,
then check it and take the first one:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[subvolumes.home]
path = "/home"
max_snapshots = 20
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol check
sudo btrfs\-patrol snapshot \-\-subvolume home
sudo btrfs\-patrol list subvolume=home
.EE
.RE
.PP
Get back one file deleted by mistake from snapshot 42 of
.IR /home ,
or roll all of
.I /home
back to it, keeping its current state as a new snapshot:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo cp \-a /.snapshots/42/snapshot/jc/Documents/report.odt ~/Documents/
sudo btrfs\-patrol rollback 42
sudo reboot
.EE
.RE
.PP
Undo an update that broke something. Snapshot 30 is the
.B dnf\-pre
snapshot taken before it; the rollback says which new snapshot keeps the
broken state, say 31:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol rollback 30 \-\-dry\-run
sudo btrfs\-patrol rollback 30
sudo reboot
.EE
.RE
.PP
After the reboot, check the restored system, and once it works, delete the
broken state to free its space \- or roll back to 31 to return to it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol check
sudo btrfs\-patrol delete 31
.EE
.RE
.PP
Recover
.I /etc
after deleting it by mistake, from a root shell that is still open (sudo
itself needs
.IR /etc ):
.PP
.RS
.EX
cp \-a /.snapshots/30/snapshot/etc /
restorecon \-R /etc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without a root shell, boot a live system, unlock and mount the top-level
subvolume, copy
.I /etc
from
.I snapshots/30/snapshot
into
.IR root ,
and on an SELinux system create
.I root/.autorelabel
before rebooting.
.
.SH CAVEATS
Snapshots are not backups: a failed disk, a lost LUKS header or a mistaken
.BR mkfs (8)
takes them too, and root can delete them. A recursive delete of
.I /
also empties writable
.B rollback
snapshots. Keep copies on another disk as well, for example with
.BR "btrfs send" .
.PP
.I /home
is a separate subvolume: it is neither snapshotted nor rolled back unless the
configuration has a
.B [subvolumes.home]
table, and then only on its own.
.PP
A release upgrade with
.B "dnf system\-upgrade"
is covered, with one limit. The download stage installs nothing and so takes no
snapshot; the offline transaction that runs at the next boot does fire the hook,
so the state from before the upgrade is kept without asking. Rolling that
snapshot back restores the older release in full, including the packages the
upgrade added and the ones it removed. But
.I /boot
is not part of the root subvolume, so the new release's kernel stays installed
while the restored root has no modules for it.
.B rollback
warns and names the kernel to choose in the boot menu; choosing the newer one
boots a kernel whose modules are gone. Until that kernel's boot entry is gone
the choice has to be made by hand at every boot, so remove the entry once the
restored system is running:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo btrfs\-patrol prune\-kernels
.EE
.RE
.PP
The boot menu then offers only kernels the restored system can run, and
.B rollback
stops warning about it.
.B "kernel\-install add\-all"
does not do this job: it enumerates kernels from
.IR /usr/lib/modules ,
which the rollback has already emptied of that version, so it cannot see the
entry that needs removing, and it rebuilds the images of every kernel it does
find, which is slow.
.PP
There are no boot menu entries for snapshots yet, so a system too broken to
log in to is recovered from a live system, as in
.BR EXAMPLES .
.
